        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Glebe%2C_New_South_Wales",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The suburb sits on a peninsula‑like ridge, with creeks such as Johnstons Creek shaping its varied micro‑relief.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/Glebe,_New_South_Wales",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Before European settlement, Glebe was covered by Sydney Turpentine‑Ironbark Forest, featuring tall grey ironbark and turpentine trees.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6,
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/Glebe,_New_South_Wales",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "A veteran ironbark tree still stands at St John’s Anglican Church on Glebe Point Road.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Glebe%2C_New_South_Wales",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "In the 1970s, activists converted a derelict terrace on Westmoreland Street into Australia’s first women’s shelter, the Elsie Refuge.",
